      "id": "3e8b596ccdd19e663c5ffa83ed569569",
      "title": "Guns, Growth, and the German Reinvention of Military Keynesianism",
      "summary": "<p>In March 2025, Germany did more than loosen a fiscal rule. It fused two national emergencies &#8212; a deteriorating European security order and a deteriorating industrial model &#8212; into a single constitutional bargain. The reform de facto exempted German defense expenditure from any domestic budgetary ceiling &#8212; as the German Bundesbank itself has acknowledged &#8212; and enabled a military expansion of extraordinary proportions. E.U. debt rules of 3 percent of GDP for annual government deficits and 60 percent for public debt technically still apply &#8212; though 14 out of the 27 Eurozone countries are not expected to meet these rules</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://warontherocks.com/guns-growth-and-the-german-reinvention-of-military-keynesianism/\">Guns, Growth, and the German Reinvention of Military Keynesianism</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://warontherocks.com\">War on the Rocks</a>.</p>",
